nts (0,a, and b). More generally, the number of idempotents is\n\nthe frequently-arising East (2010); Sloane (2021a) triangular number n(n+1)\n\n2:\n\nProposition 9 S n=M n\\ {1}has n(n+1)\n\n2 idempotents and n 3\n\n3−n\n\n3+ 1 nilpotents.\n\nProof Let N be the number of idempotents in S n=M n\\ {1}. Then N equals the\n\nnumber of idempotents in M n\\{0}, which is in turn equal to the number of elements\n\nh 0, k, m i. By Theorem 4, this number is\n\nN=\n\nn\n\nX\n\nk=1\n\nn\n\nX\n\nm=k\n\n1 = n(n+ 1)\n\n2,\n\na result which can also be found using elementary combinatorics. The number of\n\nnilpotents, which is |S n| − N+ 1, then follows from Proposition 7.\n\nProposition 9 tells us that R n contains more idempotents than does M n,\n\nsee Lemma 1. As for nilpotents, all those of R 2 belong to M 2, but this is no\n\nlonger true when n 6= 2.\n\nIn R n, any two inverse elements are transposes of one another (in the usual\n\nsense of matrix transposition), and this must also be true of the subsemi-\n\ngroup M n. With the obvious expression for h d, k, m i T, we have arrived at the\n\nfollowing proposition.\n\nProposition 10 Let x∈M n. The transpose x T of x is\n\nx T=(0, x =0,\n\nh−d, k +d, m +d i, x =h d, k, m i ∈ M n\\ {0}.(33)\n\nx T is the unique inverse of x. Therefore M n is an inverse monoid and S n is an\n\ninverse semigroup.\n\nWhile R n and S n are not abelian semigroups, some nonzero elements do\n\ncommute, e.g., e and f of S 2, see Proposition 8. What follows is a necessary\n\nand suﬃcient condition for two nonzero x, y ∈M n to commute (xy =yx).\n\nProposition 11 Let x, y ∈M n\\ {0}with x=h d, k, m i and y=h d 0, k 0, m 0 i. Then\n\n•Case 1: xy =yx 6=0 iﬀ\n\nmax(k, k 0−d) = max(k 0, k −d 0)≤min(m, m 0−d) = min(m 0, m −d 0).(34)\n\n•Case 2: xy =yx =0 iﬀ\n\nmax(k, k 0−d)>min(m, m 0−d) and max(k 0, k −d 0)>min(m 0, m −d 0).\n\n(35)\n\nSpringer Nature 2021 L\n\nA\n\nT\n\nE\n\nX template\n\nA subsemigroup of the rook monoid 13\n\nProof Apply Corollary 1, interchange the roles of x and y, and apply again. \n\nIn the special case d=d 0= 0, the conditions of either Case 1 or Case 2 are\n\nsatisﬁed for all k, m, k 0, m 0(as expected, because diagonal matrices commute,\n\nsee also Section 8.1). Thus the idempotents of M n and S n commute, which\n\nis tantamount to saying that M n and S n are orthodox semigroups. This was\n\nknown beforehand, because all inverse semigroups are orthodox.\n\n7 On the numbers of zero and nonzero products\n\nThe set S 2\\ {0}, which has 4 elements, gives rise to 4 2= 16 possible products.\n\nThe last four rows and columns of the table in Proposition 8 tell us that 8\n\nof these products are zero and 8 are nonzero. This case (n= 2) seems to be\n\nthe only one in which the numbers of zero and nonzero products are equal;\n\ne.g. S 3\\ {0}, which has 13 elements, gives rise to 91 nonzero products, about\n\nr(3) = 53.9% of the total. We have investigated the more general ratio r(n) in\n\nthe following manner.\n\nLet ψ(n) be the number of nonzero products arising from the set M n\\ {0}.\n\nBy (17) and Corollary 1,ψ(n) is given by\n\nψ(n) =\n\nn−1\n\nX\n\nd=1−n\n\nn−max(0,d)\n\nX\n\nk=1−min(0,d)\n\nn−max(0,d)\n\nX\n\nm=k\n\nn−1\n\nX\n\nd 0=1−n\n\nmin(m+d,n−max(0,d 0))\n\nX\n\nk 0=1−min(0,d 0)\n\nn−max(0,d 0)\n\nX\n\nm 0=max(k 0,k+d)\n\n1.\n\n(36)\n\nNow 2|S n|−1 of these nonzero products involve 1, so that S n\\{0}=M n\\{0,1}\n\ngives rise to ψ(n)−2|S n|+1 nonzero products. As the total number of products\n\nis (|S n| − 1)2, the ratio r(n) is\n\nr(n) = ψ(n)−2|S n|+ 1\n\n(|S n| − 1)2.(37)\n\nAlthough were not able to analytically evaluate the sextuple sum giving ψ(n),\n\nnumerical evaluation and use of the On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences\n\n(OEIS) suggested a connection to the so-called Polynexus numbers Sloane\n\n(2021c), as stated in the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 1 For n∈N,ψ(n) equals the (n+ 1)th Polynexus number of order 7\n\nSLF n={0} ∪ {h d, 1−d, n i ∈ M n\\ {0}:d < 0}.(43)\n\nAn