h root of x=h d, k, m i ∈ S UT n. Therefore (55) holds. Since\n\nboth h 1,1,1 i and h 1, k, m +d−1 i belong to A n, (55) explicitly demonstrates that\n\nA n generates SU T n.\n\nWe now turn to minimality and rank. If x i=h d i, k i, m i i ∈ SU T n\\ {0}, then\n\nP p\n\ni=1 d i≥p by (42); eqn. (53) and Theorem 3 then show that the product Q p\n\ni=1 x i\n\ncan belong to A n only if p= 1 and x 1∈A n. In other words, any y∈A n can be\n\nwritten as a ﬁnite product of elements of SU T n only in a trivial manner (i.e., the\n\nproduct can only have one factor, equal to y), implying that any generating set must\n\ncontain this y. We have thus shown that\n\n(B n⊆SU T n and B n generates SU T n) =⇒A n⊆B n.\n\nIt follows that no proper subset of A n can generate SU T n, proving minimality of\n\nthe generating set A n. It also follows that |A n| ≤ |B n|for any B n generating SUT n,\n\nso that the rank of SU T n equals the cardinality of its generating set A n. Computing\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://slimjectvitamins.com/accel-vitamin-b-complex-capsules/?_=%2Fsearch%2F%23CD3DgejCa2b3OIySVEszRQc%3D&query=Fikioris%2C+G&searchtype=author)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Comments: Accepted in EC'23 and Mathematics of Operations Research\n\n  22. arXiv:2111.01863  [pdf, other]\nSubmitted 14 May, 2024; v1 submitted 14 October, 2022; originally announced October 2022.\n\nComments: Accepted in EC'23 and Mathematics of Operations Research\n\n  22. arXiv:2111.01863  [pdf, other] \n\nmath.CO math.GR\n\nA subsemigroup of the rook monoid\n\nAuthors: George Fikioris, Giannis Fikioris\n\nAbstract: We define a subsemigroup $S_n$ of the rook monoid $R_n$ and investigate its properties. To do this, we represent the nonzero elements of $S_n$ (which are $n\\times n$ matrices) via certain triplets of integers, and develop a closed-form expression representing the product of two elements; these tools facilitate straightforward deductions of a great number of properties. For example, we show that… ▽ More We define a subsemigroup $S_n$ of the rook monoid $R_n$ and investigate its properties. To do this, we represent the nonzero elements of $S_n$ (which are $n\\times n$ matrices) via certain triplets of integers, and develop a closed-form expression representing the product of two elements; these tools facilitate straightforward deductions of a great number of properties. For example, we show that $S_n$ consists solely of idempotents and nilpotents, find the numbers of idempotents and nilpotents, and compute nilpotency indexes. Furthermore, we give a necessary and sufficient condition for the $j$th root of a nonzero element to exist in $S_n$, show that existence implies uniqueness, and compute the said root explicitly. We also point to several combinatorial aspects; describe a number of subsemigroups of $S_n$; and, using rook $n$-diagrams, graphically interpret many of our results. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 25 May, 2022; v1 submitted 2 November, 2021; originally announced November 2021.\n\nMSC Class: 20M18; 20M19\n\n  23. arXiv:2109.12401  [pdf, ps, other] \n\ncs.GT\n\nIncentives in Dominant Resource Fair Allocation under Dynamic Demands\n\nAuthors: Giannis Fikioris, Rachit Agarwal, Éva Tardos\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Fikioris_G in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AFikioris_G+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Nov 04 2021 math.CO math.GR arXiv:2111.01863v3\nWe point out similarities and find essential differences. We show that $\\overline{M}_n$ is a noncommutative, periodic, combinatorial, fundamental, completely semisimple, and strongly $E^*$-unitary inverse monoid.\n\n  * A subsemigroup of the rook monoid\n\nGeorge Fikioris, Giannis Fikioris\n\nNov 04 2021 math.CO math.GR arXiv:2111.01863v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2111.01863, author = {George Fikioris and Giannis Fikioris}, title = {{A} subsemigroup of the rook monoid}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2111.01863}, note = {arXiv:2111.01863v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe define a subsemigroup $S_n$ of the rook monoid $R_n$ and investigate its properties. To do this, we represent the nonzero elements of $S_n$ (which are $n\\times n$ matrices) via certain triplets of integers, and develop a closed-form expression representing the product of two elements; these tools facilitate straightforward deductions of a great number of properties. For example, we show that $S_n$ consists solely of idempotents and nilpotents, find the numbers of idempotents and nilpotents, and compute nilpotency indexes. Furthermore, we give a necessary and sufficient condition for the $j$th root of a nonzero element to exist in $S_n$, show that existence implies uniqueness, and compute the said root explicitly. We also point to several combinatorial aspects; describe a number of subsemigroups of $S_n$; and, using rook $n$-diagrams, graphically interpret many of our results.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Pritchard94/polymarket-backend · GitHub (https://github.com/Pritchard94/polymarket-backend)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # PolyNexus Backend\n\nPritchard94 / polymarket-backend Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 0\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 0\n  * Pull requests 0\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\nmaster\n\nBranchesTags\n\n## Latest commit\n\n \n\n## History\n\n18 Commits\n\n## Folders and files\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nsrc  | src  |    |  \n.gitignore  | .gitignore  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \npackage-lock.json  | package-lock.json  |    |  \npackage.json  | package.json  |    |  \n\n# PolyNexus Backend\n\nAlways-alive Node.js backend for the PolyNexus Polymarket Tracker. Runs 24/7 on Railway, monitoring the Polymarket WebSocket feed for new Yes/No markets and sending Discord notifications to all registered users.\n    }\n\n## Running Locally\n    \n    npm install\n    npm run dev\n\n## Deploying to Railway\n\n  1. Push this folder to a separate GitHub repository (e.g. `polymarket-backend`).\n  2. Go to railway.app and click New Project → Deploy from GitHub Repo.\n  3. Select your backend repository.\n  4. Railway will auto-detect Node.js and run `npm start`.\n  5. Add environment variables in the Railway dashboard:\n     * `FRONTEND_URL` → your Vercel frontend URL (e.g. `https://polynexus.vercel.app`)\n  6. Copy the Railway public URL (e.g. `https://polymarket-backend.railway.app`).\n  7. Add it to your Vercel frontend as `VITE_API_URL`.\n\n## Notes\n\n  * User data is currently in-memory and resets on server restart. For production, add a Railway PostgreSQL or MongoDB addon.\n  * The server connects to `wss://ws-subscriptions-clob.polymarket.com/ws/market` on startup and auto-reconnects on disconnect.\n  * It also polls `https://gamma-api.polymarket.com/markets` every 60 seconds as a fallback.\n\n## About\n\nNo description, website, or topics provided.\n\n### Stars\n\n0 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks\n\n## Releases\n\n## Packages\n\n## Contributors\n\n## Languages\n"
